From f80c711ecb4e95feb59bbc38fbca770d4b9630f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: danishistired <iamdanishverma@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 13:59:56 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] blt-toasty: validate Slack message values; label severity
 an estimate (#35)

Review follow-up on PR #8.

Trust boundary: every value interpolated into Slack mrkdwn is now
checked against its expected format before use. Issue numbers must be
positive integers, event IDs must match [A-Za-z0-9_:-]{1,64}, CWE IDs
CWE-<digits> or CWE-UNKNOWN, scores finite numbers in [0, 1]. Anything
else becomes "unknown issue" / "[invalid id]" / "similarity n/a" or is
dropped. All interpolated text is also escaped (&, <, >), so a crafted
value cannot form a Slack link, mention or formatting.

Severity: the CWE table is a heuristic, so the field now reads
"Est. severity (from CWE type)" with a note that it is not an
assessment of the report. CWEs the table does not know (and missing or
CWE-UNKNOWN) give "unrated, needs review" instead of a guessed tier.
notify_triage/build_payload accept an optional assessed_severity; a
valid tier is shown as "Severity (assessed)" instead of the estimate,
so a future assessment stage is picked up without notifier changes.

Tests: a SQLite-backed D1 fixture (conftest.sqlite_d1) applies the
real migrations and runs the notifier's queries, replacing the mock
that routed by SQL text. New crafted-input tests cover each
interpolated field, including crafted values stored in D1.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
